Add CRYPTO_thread_local_cleanup() to release the BoringSSL thread-local TLS
slot when the consuming DLL is unloaded before process exit.

BoringSSL holds a process-wide TlsAlloc()'d slot for the lifetime of the
process. That model is wrong when BoringSSL is statically linked into a
DLL that gets loaded and unloaded inside a longer-lived host (e.g.
pagespeed_iis.dll inside an IIS w3wp.exe worker). AppVerifier flags the
unfreed slot at FreeLibrary as AVRF_TLS_LEAK; absent the verifier, every
DLL recycle quietly leaks one TLS index.

This patch adds a small extern "C" cleanup function the host can call from
its DllMain DLL_PROCESS_DETACH path. After the call BoringSSL's
thread-local APIs become permanent no-ops in this process, which is the
correct behavior since the DLL is on its way out.

Motivation: an IIS native module (pagespeed_iis.dll) must release
BoringSSL's process-wide TLS slot on DLL unload; without this entry point
the slot leaks and thread-local state dangles.

--- a/crypto/thread_win.cc
+++ b/crypto/thread_win.cc
@@ -68,6 +68,20 @@ static void thread_local_init(void) {
   g_thread_local_failed = (g_thread_local_key == TLS_OUT_OF_INDEXES);
 }

+// Release the process-wide TLS slot allocated lazily by thread_local_init.
+// Intended for hosts that load BoringSSL inside a DLL that gets unloaded
+// before process exit (e.g. an IIS native module). After this call, the
+// per-thread BoringSSL APIs become no-ops in this process. Safe to call
+// even if thread_local_init never ran.
+extern "C" void CRYPTO_thread_local_cleanup(void) {
+  if (g_thread_local_failed) {
+    return;
+  }
+  TlsFree(g_thread_local_key);
+  g_thread_local_key = TLS_OUT_OF_INDEXES;
+  g_thread_local_failed = 1;
+}
+
 static void NTAPI thread_local_destructor(PVOID module, DWORD reason,
                                           PVOID reserved) {
